UID:camargo-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090441Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: Camargo Fellowship 2027-2028 for Artists\, Scholars and T
 hinkers (Cassis\, France)
DESCRIPTION:The Camargo Foundation invites artists\, scholars and thinkers 
 to its flagship Core Fellowship: a ten-week self-directed residency on its
  clifftop campus overlooking the Mediterranean in Cassis\, France\, dedica
 ted to research\, experimentation and interdisciplinary exchange. Curators
  apply under the Thinkers category\, which covers accomplished professiona
 ls and practitioners in cultural and creative fields who are professionall
 y engaged in critical thought\, including curators\, critics and journalis
 ts\; projects should engage theoretical\, artistic or societal questions. 
 WHAT YOU GET: a stipend of EUR 350 per week (EUR 3\,500 total)\, basic tra
 nsportation funding\, and a furnished apartment with an equipped kitchen o
 n campus. Fourteen fellowships are awarded per year: seven for artists and
  seven for scholars and thinkers. RESIDENCY PERIODS: Fall 2027 (8 Septembe
 r to 17 November 2027) or Spring 2028 (23 February to 4 May 2028). ELIGIBI
 LITY: artists in visual arts\, performance\, writing\, film\, music or mul
 tidisciplinary work with at least 5 years of professional practice\; schol
 ars holding or pursuing a PhD focused on French and Francophone cultures o
 r cross-cultural Mediterranean studies\; and thinkers as described above. 
 APPLICATION: via Submittable with a 100-word proposal summary\, a 1\,200-w
 ord proposal narrative\, a 300-word rationale for Camargo\, a 300-word car
 eer-stage rationale\, a CV (max 3 pages)\, work samples and two references
 . NOTE: the foundation has announced that this is the final Fellowship sel
 ected through a public open call until further notice\; a new selection pr
 ocess begins in July 2027\, making this the last chance to apply directly.
 \n\nOrganisation: Camargo Foundation\nLocation: Camargo Foundation campus\
 , Cassis\, France. Open worldwide.\nAward: EUR 350 per week (EUR 3\,500 to
 tal for the ten-week residency)\, plus basic travel funding and a furnishe
 d apartment with equipped kitchen on the Camargo campus. 14 fellowships pe
 r year: 7 for artists and 7 for scholars and thinkers. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Ten weeks\, either 8 September to 17 November 2027 or 23 Febru
 ary to 4 May 2028.\n\nMore: https://camargofoundation.org/en/camargo-fello
 wship-2026-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://camargofoundation.org/en/camargo-fellowship-2026-2027?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Camargo Foundation campus\, Cassis\, France. Open worldwide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:khk-cure-fellowships-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090441Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261130
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261201
SUMMARY:Deadline: KHK CURE Fellowships 2027/2028\, Saarland University (the
 me: Things)
DESCRIPTION:The Kaete Hamburger Centre for Cultural Practices of Reparation
  (KHK CURE) at Saarland University invites applications for up to twelve f
 ellowships for its fourth year\, October 2027 to September 2028. CURE welc
 omes scholars from cultural studies\, the humanities and the social scienc
 es whose work examines questions of reparation and irreparability\; artist
 s holding a doctorate and experienced in academic collaboration may also a
 pply. ANNUAL THEME: 'Things'\, foregrounding the material dimension of rep
 aration: objects\, artefacts and material environments through which pract
 ices such as repair\, mending and reuse acquire cultural and political sig
 nificance. Proposals must align with at least one of the Centre's three th
 ematic fields (history\, experience or nature) and address the annual them
 e. CONDITIONS: fellowships run six to twelve months from 1 October 2027\; 
 fellows on unpaid leave receive approximately EUR 6\,100 to 7\,200 per mon
 th before taxes depending on qualifications\, or the home institution is r
 eimbursed for replacement teaching\; one round trip to Saarbruecken is rei
 mbursed under the Saarland Travel Expense Act\; a fully equipped individua
 l workspace is provided and an apartment can be arranged for around EUR 1\
 ,250 per month\; fellows must live in Saarbruecken and take part in the Ce
 ntre's international\, interdisciplinary research community. ELIGIBILITY: 
 a completed PhD (submitted and defended) by the deadline\; retired persons
  and Saarland University staff are not eligible. HOW TO APPLY: via the onl
 ine form with a one-page motivation letter\, CV (max 3 pages)\, list of pu
 blications\, and a five-page project description with bibliography\, in En
 glish\, German or French. Notification by 30 April 2027. Deadline 30 Novem
 ber 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Kaete Hamburger Centre for Cultural Practices o
 f Reparation (CURE)\, Saarland University\nLocation: Saarbruecken\, German
 y. Fellows must live in Saarbruecken during the fellowship and participate
  actively in centre events\; extended research absences are not permitted.
  Open internationally\; retired persons and Saarland University staff are 
 not eligible.\nAward: Up to twelve fellowships. Fellows on unpaid leave fr
 om their institution receive approximately EUR 6\,100 to 7\,200 per month 
 before taxes\, depending on qualifications\; alternatively\, fellows retai
 ning their salary can have their home institution reimbursed for replaceme
 nt teaching. One round trip to Saarbruecken is reimbursed\, a fully equipp
 ed individual workspace is provided\, and an apartment can be arranged for
  around EUR 1\,250 per month. Eligible accompanying family members may rec
 eive additional support. No application fee.\nDuration: Six to twelve mont
 hs\, beginning 1 October 2027\n\nMore: https://cure.uni-saarland.de/en/fel
 lows/call-for-applications?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_mediu
 m=referral
URL:https://cure.uni-saarland.de/en/fellows/call-for-applications?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Saarbruecken\, Germany. Fellows must live in Saarbruecken during t
 he fellowship and participate actively in centre events\; extended researc
 h absences are not permitted. Open internationally\; retired persons and S
 aarland University staff are not eligible.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:culture360-asef-arts-in-the-wake-of-ai-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090441Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260929
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260930
SUMMARY:Deadline: culture360 / ASEF: Call for Content\, Arts in the Wake of
  AI
DESCRIPTION:culture360.ASEF.org\, the arts platform of the Asia-Europe Foun
 dation\, invites proposals from writers and content producers for its open
  call 'Arts in the Wake of AI': articles\, podcasts and video interviews t
 hat foreground how cultural professionals and organisations across Asia an
 d Europe are adapting to AI technologies\, from generative tools and machi
 ne learning to recommender systems and algorithms. WHAT THEY WANT: case-st
 udy-based contributions grounded in real-world evidence\, experiences or p
 ractices\, on topics including the impact of AI on cultural diversity\, fa
 irness and inclusivity\; the environmental costs of AI in the cultural sec
 tor\; and AI governance in the cultural sector (policy\, copyright\, intel
 lectual property and the ethical use of AI-generated cultural content). Ac
 ademic research papers and non-arts subjects are not considered. FEE: SGD 
 250 per commissioned article\, podcast or video interview. FORMATS: journa
 listic articles of 800 to 1\,000 words with at least four credited images\
 ; podcasts and video interviews of 15 to 20 minutes. ELIGIBILITY: national
 s of ASEM partner countries with previous experience in writing\, podcasti
 ng or video production\, proficiency in English\, and knowledge of or invo
 lvement in the Asian and European arts and culture scene\; an existing cha
 nnel for further dissemination is an advantage\; the work is fully virtual
 . Up to two proposals per application. Only selected writers are notified.
  Queries to culture[at]asef.org. Deadline 29 September 2026 at 6 PM Singap
 ore time\, via the online application form.\n\nOrganisation: Asia-Europe F
 oundation (ASEF)\, culture360.ASEF.org\nLocation: Fully remote\; contribut
 ors do not need to be based in Singapore. Open to nationals of ASEM partne
 r countries across Asia and Europe.\nAward: SGD 250 fee per commissioned a
 rticle\, podcast or video interview. Up to two proposals per application\,
  of which at most one is selected. No application fee.\nDuration: One-off 
 editorial commissions: articles of 800 to 1\,000 words with at least four 
 credited pictures\, podcasts and video interviews of 15 to 20 minutes\n\nM
 ore: https://culture360.asef.org/opportunities/call-for-content-arts-ai/?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://culture360.asef.org/opportunities/call-for-content-arts-ai/?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Fully remote\; contributors do not need to be based in Singapore. 
 Open to nationals of ASEM partner countries across Asia and Europe.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:heavens-hospital-next-nature-open-call-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090441Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261020
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261021
SUMMARY:Deadline: Next Nature: Heaven's Hospital Open Call (commissions on 
 the future of care)
DESCRIPTION:Heaven's Hospital\, a research and presentation platform and ex
 hibition at the Next Nature Museum\, invites designers\, artists\, archite
 cts\, filmmakers\, writers\, technologists\, game developers\, researchers
 \, scientists\, care professionals and other creative makers to develop ne
 w perspectives on the future of care. Starting from the question of how ca
 re environments can transform from repair factories into sensory ecosystem
 s\, the open call The Dreamed-Up Care seeks fresh ideas rather than ready-
 made solutions: speculative designs\, artistic research\, future scenarios
 \, prototypes\, new applications of AI\, architecture\, digital systems\, 
 rituals\, films\, installations\, services and new forms of collaboration.
  TWELVE CARE ISSUES frame the call\, including the shift from illness to h
 ealth\, the hospital as machine\, staff shortages\, AI changing the role o
 f the doctor\, the patient as data producer\, chronic conditions\, mental 
 health\, the missing human experience in hospitals\, care inequality\, cli
 mate and care\, fragmentation of treatment\, and the medicalisation of bir
 th\, old age and dying. WHAT THEY WANT: projects exploring the future of h
 ealthcare through artistic research\, speculative design or technological 
 innovation that actively engage the public (installations\, interactive en
 vironments\, spatial interventions\, digital experiences\, games\, perform
 ances or other participatory forms)\, and cross-disciplinary work combinin
 g design with AI\, robotics\, immersive media\, biotechnology\, synthetic 
 biology\, architecture\, food design\, sound design\, data science\, ecolo
 gy or philosophy. SUPPORT: three selected projects each receive EUR 15\,00
 0 to develop a first prototype or artistic intervention\, become Heaven's 
 Hospital Fellows\, contribute to the ongoing research programme and may be
  publicly presented in the Next Nature Museum. Proposals must be feasible 
 within the available time and budget while remaining ambitious and experim
 ental. WHO CAN APPLY: individuals or collectives. HOW TO APPLY: one propos
 al per participant via the online application portal\, consisting of the c
 ompleted application form\, a CV and a portfolio of maximum 10 pages or sl
 ides\; PDF only\, maximum 4 MB per file\, in English or Dutch. SELECTION C
 RITERIA: originality and imagination\, relevance to the future of healthca
 re\, artistic and design quality\, innovative use of technology\, public e
 ngagement\, feasibility\, and potential contribution to the research progr
 amme. TIMELINE: deadline 20 October 2026\; jury meets November 2026\; sele
 cted participants announced December 2026\; research\, development and pro
 totype production through 2027.\n\nOrganisation: Heaven's Hospital / Next 
 Nature Museum\nLocation: Next Nature Museum\, Eindhoven\, the Netherlands.
  Open internationally\; applications in English or Dutch.\nAward: Three se
 lected projects each receive a budget of EUR 15\,000 for the development o
 f a first prototype or artistic intervention. Selected participants become
  Heaven's Hospital Fellows and join an international network of designers\
 , researchers\, care professionals and artists. No application fee stated.
 \nDuration: Research\, development and prototype production during 2027\, 
 with presentation within the Heaven's Hospital exhibition at the Next Natu
 re Museum.\n\nMore: https://nextnature.org/en/opencall?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://nextnature.org/en/opencall?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Next Nature Museum\, Eindhoven\, the Netherlands. Open internation
 ally\; applications in English or Dutch.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ctm-resynthesising-traditional-at-the-extreme-2027@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090441Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260928
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260929
SUMMARY:Deadline: CTM Festival: Resynthesising the Traditional 2027 Artisti
 c Lab (At the Extreme)
DESCRIPTION:Resynthesising the Traditional is CTM Festival's long-term init
 iative co-curated by Stas Sharifulla\, taking resynthesis (the computer-mu
 sic method of analysing a sound to extract its components) as a metaphor f
 or critically engaging with tradition and heritage\, against conservative 
 views of culture as frozen and untouchable. The artistic lab at its heart 
 is an open toolbox for artists connecting to the traditional outside exoti
 cisation\, cultural essentialism and uneven power dynamics. THEME\, AT THE
  EXTREME: this edition invites artists and practice-based researchers whos
 e work examines the ethical\, aesthetic and political implications of trad
 itions entangled with extremity: intensity\, transgression\, transformatio
 n\, marginality\, outsiderness\, endurance\, conflict and excess. The call
  explicitly looks past the usual noise\, violence and loud-popular-music t
 ropes and past masculine codings of the extreme\, toward current queer and
  feminist reframings\, treating extremity as a relational condition\, chos
 en or imposed\, produced between majoritarian centres and their minoritari
 an outsides. Guiding questions include what it means to follow a tradition
  to its extreme consequences\, who is allowed to push tradition to its lim
 its and whose practices get framed as inauthentic or dangerous\, what it m
 eans to practise tradition under war\, displacement\, repression or ecolog
 ical crisis\, and how traditions might offer ways of inhabiting extreme ti
 mes. Preference goes to work engaging technological\, bodily\, aesthetic a
 nd theoretical forms at that intersection\, especially in unexpected or ra
 rely recognised ways. WHAT HAPPENS: theory and practice workshops from the
  co-hosts with individual and group exercises\, closed 15 to 20 minute ora
 l presentations by each participant\, a solo public performance or present
 ation (15 to 20 minutes) at a free-entry event\, inputs from specialists s
 uch as artists\, researchers and technologists\, guided individual and gro
 up sonic improvisations\, field trips and site visits\, and feedback sessi
 ons with the co-hosts and curators. AFTERWARDS: participants may propose n
 ew artistic work or research\, solo or in collaborations formed at the lab
 \, to the artistic team\; selected works are shortlisted\, and up to two a
 re commissioned and presented at CTM in January/February 2028. ELIGIBILITY
 : an existing practice tied to (re)connecting with traditional ideas and s
 ounds through a contemporary lens and how that overlaps with the extreme\,
  plus clear ideas or questions about the next step you want to take. Appro
 aches may be technological\, instrument-focused\, compositional\, aestheti
 c\, research-oriented or otherwise. Working knowledge of English is recomm
 ended\, and any concerns about that can be flagged in the form. Up to six 
 participants are selected by co-hosts Karina Utomo (extreme metal vocalist
 \, composer and collaborative artist) and Luigi Monteanni (sound researche
 r and ethnomusicologist completing a PhD at SOAS on extreme metal in Bandu
 ng). The lab is a time-intensive full-day commitment and participants must
  agree to give the short public performance. APPLY through the form with a
  portfolio link (PDF\, max 10 pages\, public access\, works from the last 
 five years\, text kept minimal) and a link to a specific audio track or se
 ction (max five minutes) that best represents your current practice. CTM a
 sks that AI support rather than replace your own voice in the application.
  TIMELINE: deadline 28 September 2026\; participants announced mid to late
  October 2026. Questions to resynth@ctm-festival.de.\n\nOrganisation: CTM 
 Festival\, co-curated by Stas Sharifulla\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany. Open
  to artists worldwide\; solo artists and projects only.\nAward: Travel bud
 get of up to EUR 300 per person\, plus accommodation and catering for the 
 duration of the lab and a festival pass to CTM 2027 events. Visa fees are 
 supported where required\, and letters of invitation are provided for visa
  or funding applications. NO artist fee or stipend for the lab itself. Aft
 erwards\, up to two new works arising from the lab may be commissioned and
  presented at CTM Festival in January/February 2028. No application fee.\n
 Duration: Seven days: arrival 24 January\, lab 26 to 31 January 2027 (10:0
 0 to 19:00 daily)\, public presentations 26 January\, departure 1 February
  2027.\n\nMore: https://www.ctm-festival.de/festival-2027/open-calls/resyn
 thesising-the-traditional-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
URL:https://www.ctm-festival.de/festival-2027/open-calls/resynthesising-the
 -traditional-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany. Open to artists worldwide\; solo artists and pro
 jects only.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:sphera-media-lab-2026-open-call-discourse@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090441Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Sphera Media Lab 2026: Open Call for Masterclasses and Ke
 ynotes
DESCRIPTION:European Lab and the Sphera Network are opening submissions for
  the discourse programme of the next Sphera Media Lab\, the annual forum f
 or people defending independent media in Europe. THEMES: technological ind
 ependence (sovereignty\, tools\, mutualisation)\; economic independence (c
 ooperative models)\; editorial independence (press freedom\, resisting the
  far right). FORMATS SOUGHT: masterclasses\, led by an expert sharing adva
 nced knowledge\, technique and hands-on insight\; and keynotes\, inspiring
  talks grounded in a specific case study. WHO CAN PROPOSE: media outlets\,
  associations\, researchers and professionals. The organisers state they p
 ay particular attention to parity and diversity across their programmes an
 d ask that proposals take those criteria into account. WHAT IS COVERED: se
 lected contributors travel to Brussels with transport\, accommodation and 
 a fee covered. THE EVENT: Sphera Media Lab 2026 runs on 3 December 2026 at
  LaVallee in Brussels\, a full day of conferences\, masterclasses and work
 shops for independent media professionals from across Europe\, alongside t
 he premiere of a documentary on threats facing European journalists. APPLI
 CATION: submit your masterclass or keynote proposal via the Typeform at ar
 ty-farty.typeform.com/to/xnDikGGM before 31 August 2026.\n\nOrganisation: 
 European Lab (Arty Farty) and the Sphera Network\, coordinated by Medianes
 \nLocation: LaVallee\, Rue Adolphe Lavallee 39\, 1080 Brussels\, Belgium. 
 Proposals are accepted regardless of where you are based\, but they should
  reflect a European perspective.\nAward: Selected contributors receive a f
 ee plus travel to Brussels and accommodation\, all covered by the organise
 rs. The fee amount is not published in the call. No submission fee.\nDurat
 ion: One-day event on 3 December 2026\; contributors deliver a single mast
 erclass or keynote\n\nMore: https://arty-farty.typeform.com/to/xnDikGGM?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://arty-farty.typeform.com/to/xnDikGGM?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:LaVallee\, Rue Adolphe Lavallee 39\, 1080 Brussels\, Belgium. Prop
 osals are accepted regardless of where you are based\, but they should ref
 lect a European perspective.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:darc-transmediale-interstices-research-workshop-2027@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090441Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: transmediale x DARC: Interstices\, Research (in) the Gaps
 \, PhD/Research Workshop
DESCRIPTION:The annual DARC PhD/Research Workshop\, organised by the Digita
 l Aesthetics Research Center at Aarhus University with transmediale\, resp
 onds to transmediale's 2027 thematic framework. THEME: 'Interstices: Resea
 rch (in) the gaps'. An interstice is the small space between objects set c
 losely together\, the cords in a rope or the atoms in a crystal lattice: a
 n emptiness produced by the structure it belongs to\, and without which th
 at structure could not be what it is. In crystallography\, interstitial si
 tes are the spaces a lattice and its basis generate but do not fill\, and 
 interstitial diffusion\, the movement of small atoms through the gaps of a
  host lattice\, is the mechanism by which materials transform. The call as
 ks what the equivalent sites are in social\, cultural and computational st
 ructures: where are the gaps these combinations generate but do not fill\,
  what moves through them\, and do they carry transformative power? It work
 s through Yancey Strickler's dark forest theory of the internet (the withd
 rawal of meaningful exchange from the open\, surveilled\, indexed web into
  private channels)\, the Dark Forest Collective's anthology and its 'cozyw
 eb' of small sheltered spaces\, dark nets\, miniverses and moving castles 
 as interstitial spaces produced by and defined against the dominant web\, 
 and Bogna Konior's extension of the dark forest metaphor to the opacity of
  AI systems\, where silence is refusal rather than absence and the forest 
 is increasingly inhabited by other-than-human intelligences that opt out o
 f engagement with people. Where big tech pursues efficiency through the au
 tomation of human perception\, cognition\, identification and aesthetics\,
  artists and researchers explore the non/more-than-human\, glitchy\, noisy
 \, machinic\, datafied and dark to build new expression and critique of da
 ta-ism and what is marketed as artificial intelligence\, including the dan
 gers and potentials inside badly curated data models\, between polluting d
 ata centres\, and in skeuomorphic interfaces. WHAT THEY WANT: proposals fr
 om artists\, researchers and practitioners for work that occupies and move
 s through gaps that are social as well as structural\, ontological and mat
 erial. The gaps are physical too: the heat of data centres and their thirs
 t for water\, latency in the cables\, unindexed nodes. The call invites sp
 eculations\, practical interventions and inferences that fill the gaps and
  expand the diversity of possible communications and communities that can 
 inhabit dark forests\, including those built on\, against and beneath the 
 infrastructures underlying them\, and welcomes work on the (dis-)continuou
 s histories of technological exploration of computational infrastructures 
 over the last 30 years or longer\, and on how current artistic\, theoretic
 al and technical explorations repeat or break from them. WHO: PhD candidat
 es and early-career researchers\, with submissions welcome from researcher
 s inside and outside the academy\, including artists\, designers and curat
 ors. PARTICIPANT EXPECTATIONS: participants write a paper and present it a
 t the workshop\, and on the basis of that peer review shorten the research
  into a newspaper-length article for distribution at the transmediale fest
 ival. The workshop is led by scholars from DARC at Aarhus University. DEAD
 LINE: 28 August 2026\, 23:59 CEST\, via the application link on the call p
 age. Queries to Pablo Velasco\, pvelasco[at]cc.au.dk.\n\nOrganisation: Dig
 ital Aesthetics Research Center (DARC)\, Aarhus University\, in collaborat
 ion with transmediale\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\; the workshop runs alon
 gside transmediale 2027 (festival 26 to 31 January 2027). Open to research
 ers based anywhere\, inside or outside the academy.\nAward: No submission 
 fee. The published call does not state a participation fee\, honorarium\, 
 travel or accommodation support\, so confirm with the organisers before bu
 dgeting. Accepted participants present a paper at the workshop and their c
 ondensed text is distributed as a newspaper-style article at the festival.
 \nDuration: Research workshop held around transmediale 2027 in Berlin (fes
 tival 26 to 31 January 2027)\n\nMore: https://transmediale.de/en/news/open
 -call-for-darc-phd-research-workshop-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://transmediale.de/en/news/open-call-for-darc-phd-research-worksho
 p-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\; the workshop runs alongside transmediale 2027 (
 festival 26 to 31 January 2027). Open to researchers based anywhere\, insi
 de or outside the academy.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mondriaan-international-collaborative-projects-heritage-institutes-2026
 @artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090441Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260910
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260911
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mondriaan Fund: International Collaborative Projects with
  Heritage Institutes (September 2026 round)
DESCRIPTION:This scheme funds cultural heritage institutions in the Netherl
 ands and the Caribbean part of the Kingdom that want to collaborate with f
 oreign heritage institutions on research projects in shared collection are
 as\, including art historical and provenance research. A development phase
  can also be funded\, covering travel\, accommodation and presentation cos
 ts while the collaboration is being shaped. CONDITIONS: the applicant inst
 itution must be publicly accessible and manage nationally significant cult
 ural heritage\; it must partner with comparable institutions abroad\; the 
 research must be presented by both the Dutch and the international partner
 s\; the applicant must contribute significantly to financing the project\;
  and the international partners' share of costs must be proportional to th
 e Mondriaan Fund grant. AMOUNTS: up to 40% of variable project costs for t
 he main project\, or up to 70% of variable costs capped at EUR 15\,000 for
  development research. APPLY through the Mondriaan Fund online application
  system after registering an account. Deadline 10 September 2026 before 16
 :00 Dutch time / 10:00 Caribbean time (an earlier 28 May 2026 round has pa
 ssed).\n\nOrganisation: Mondriaan Fund\nLocation: Applicant institutions m
 ust be based in the Netherlands or the Caribbean part of the Kingdom\; the
  partner institutions are abroad.\nAward: Determined per application. Up t
 o 40% of variable project costs. For a development research phase\, up to 
 70% of variable costs capped at EUR 15\,000\, covering travel\, accommodat
 ion and presentation costs. The applicant must contribute significantly to
  the project financing\, and the international partners' cost share must b
 e proportional to the Mondriaan Fund grant. No application fee.\nDuration:
  Project-based research collaboration. Two annual rounds\; this card cover
 s the 10 September 2026 deadline.\n\nMore: https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/e
 n/apply-for-a-grant/grants/international-collaborative-projects-with-herit
 age-institutes/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/international
 -collaborative-projects-with-heritage-institutes/?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Applicant institutions must be based in the Netherlands or the Car
 ibbean part of the Kingdom\; the partner institutions are abroad.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mondriaan-heritage-innovation-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090441Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260917
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260918
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mondriaan Fund: Heritage Innovation (September 2026 round
 )
DESCRIPTION:Heritage Innovation funds projects and research tracks that con
 tribute to substantive innovation in the heritage sector. Examples given b
 y the fund include developing new narratives about cultural heritage\, str
 engthening regional collaboration across the sector\, redesigning how a co
 llection is presented\, and feasibility studies on technological applicati
 ons such as AI or gamification\, which makes this one of the more tech-adj
 acent Mondriaan schemes. WHO CAN APPLY: museums and collection-holding cul
 tural heritage institutions\, including archives\, in the Netherlands and 
 the Caribbean part of the Kingdom. Cultural organisations in the heritage 
 sector may also apply if they collaborate with one or more collection-hold
 ing institutions. All applicants must be non-profit\, not-for-profit entit
 ies. NOT FUNDED: fixed costs\, restoration\, building museums\, or regular
  operations. CONDITIONS: institutions must subscribe to the Fair Practice 
 Code\, the Culture Governance Code and the Diversity and Inclusion Code\, 
 and must pay visual artists fairly where artists are involved. AMOUNTS: pr
 ojects get up to 40% of variable project-related costs on a minimum projec
 t budget of EUR 25\,000\; research tracks get up to 70% of costs capped at
  EUR 15\,000 with no minimum budget. APPLY through the Mondriaan Fund onli
 ne system after registering an account. Deadline 17 September 2026 before 
 16:00 Dutch time / 10:00 Caribbean time.\n\nOrganisation: Mondriaan Fund\n
 Location: Museums and collection-holding heritage institutions in the Neth
 erlands and the Caribbean part of the Kingdom.\nAward: Projects: up to 40%
  of variable project-related costs\, with a minimum project budget of EUR 
 25\,000. Research tracks: up to 70% of costs\, capped at EUR 15\,000\, wit
 h no minimum budget. No application fee.\nDuration: Project or research tr
 ack. This card covers the 17 September 2026 deadline.\n\nMore: https://www
 .mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/heritage-innovation/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.mondriaanfonds.nl/en/apply-for-a-grant/grants/heritage-inno
 vation/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Museums and collection-holding heritage institutions in the Nether
 lands and the Caribbean part of the Kingdom.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stimuleringsfonds-videotage-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090441Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261021
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261022
SUMMARY:Deadline: Stimuleringsfonds: Open Call Residency Videotage 2027 (Ho
 ng Kong)
DESCRIPTION:The Creative Industries Fund NL (Stimuleringsfonds) runs this r
 esidency with Videotage\, a Hong Kong-based non-profit media arts institut
 ion specialising in the promotion\, presentation\, creation and preservati
 on of moving image and media art in all forms and languages. The Fund is r
 ecruiting one Netherlands-based designer\, maker or observer working in di
 gital culture to spend two or three months at Videotage's studio space in 
 Hong Kong in 2027\, conducting research\, broadening their network\, devel
 oping new work inspired by Hong Kong\, and presenting their findings or co
 mpleted works. The residency encourages crossovers and experimentation\, g
 iving the participant space to explore new methodologies\, widen their cre
 ative practice and produce work that pushes the boundaries of digital cult
 ure\, with a focus on social and urban dynamics. The 2027 edition takes th
 e technological 'black box' phenomenon as its starting point and invites t
 he resident to respond to that theme within the context of Videotage. ELIG
 IBILITY: you are an individual designer\, maker or observer within the fie
 ld of digital culture\; you are established in the Netherlands\, Aruba\, B
 onaire\, Curacao\, Saba\, Sint Eustatius or Sint Maarten (Kingdom of the N
 etherlands)\; you are registered in the business register of the Dutch Cha
 mber of Commerce (KvK) or another business register within the Kingdom of 
 the Netherlands\; and you have had a professional practice in the creative
  industry for at least four years. NOT open to duos\, collectives or coope
 rative ventures\, nor to designers and makers who receive a grant during t
 he work period under the Talent Development Grant Scheme\, the Open Call S
 elf-taught Designer or the Open Call Emerging Design Practices ABCSSS. Not
 e that accepting this open call means you cannot apply to those schemes an
 d open calls in 2027. FUNDING: EUR 5\,000 for two months or EUR 6\,500 for
  three months\, covering travel\, insurance\, living expenses\, compensati
 on for time invested and material and transport costs\; accommodation\, st
 udio use and Videotage's facilitation are covered separately by the Fund. 
 Submit your proposal to the Creative Industries Fund NL by 21 October 2026
 \, 16:00 CEST.\n\nOrganisation: Stimuleringsfonds Creatieve Industrie (Cre
 ative Industries Fund NL) in collaboration with Videotage\, Hong Kong\nLoc
 ation: Residency takes place at Videotage in Hong Kong. Applicants must be
  established in the Netherlands\, Aruba\, Bonaire\, Curacao\, Saba\, Sint 
 Eustatius or Sint Maarten (Kingdom of the Netherlands) and registered in a
  business register within the Kingdom of the Netherlands.\nAward: EUR 5\,0
 00 for a two-month residency or EUR 6\,500 for a three-month residency\, p
 aid by the Creative Industries Fund NL to cover travel\, insurance\, livin
 g expenses\, compensation for time invested\, and material and transport c
 osts. Accommodation\, use of the studio space and facilitation by Videotag
 e are covered directly by the Fund on top of this. No application fee.\nDu
 ration: Two or three months in 2027 (spring 2027)\; state your preferred p
 eriod in the application.\n\nMore: https://www.stimuleringsfonds.nl/en/gra
 nts/open-call-videotage?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
URL:https://www.stimuleringsfonds.nl/en/grants/open-call-videotage?utm_sour
 ce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Residency takes place at Videotage in Hong Kong. Applicants must b
 e established in the Netherlands\, Aruba\, Bonaire\, Curacao\, Saba\, Sint
  Eustatius or Sint Maarten (Kingdom of the Netherlands) and registered in 
 a business register within the Kingdom of the Netherlands.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ars-biologica-artxscience-residency-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090441Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260928
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260929
SUMMARY:Deadline: Ars Biologica ArtXScience Residency 2027-2028 (Ars Electr
 onica x Ceske Budejovice ECoC 2028)
DESCRIPTION:A fully funded art-science residency from Ars Electronica and C
 eske Budejovice\, European Capital of Culture 2028\, exploring how art and
  science can collaborate on urgent environmental challenges: climate emerg
 ence\, sustainable agriculture\, geoengineering\, permaculture\, water res
 ource management\, and the broader ecological impacts on local communities
 . Selected residents work in direct collaboration with local scientists in
  Ceske Budejovice. WHO CAN APPLY: artists\, designers\, creatives and arti
 st collectives with more than 5 years of professional creative practice an
 d documented interdisciplinary work\; experience in community engagement a
 nd science collaborations is essential\; applicants must be 18+\, of any n
 ationality\, and legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. WHAT YOU G
 ET: a EUR 24\,000 package (EUR 6\,000 artist fee\, up to EUR 15\,000 produ
 ction\, up to EUR 3\,000 travel and subsistence) plus one month of housing
  covered separately\; two winners are selected. STRUCTURE: the residency r
 uns January 2027 to April 2028\, with a required on-site stay of up to one
  month in February/March 2027\, an optional stay in August/September 2027\
 , and a March 2028 stay for festival installation\, exhibition and deinsta
 llation. HOW TO APPLY: online form with project title\, a written proposal
  of max 250 words\, a scientific direction statement (max 100 words)\, art
 istic vision (max 100 words)\, community connection plan (max 100 words)\,
  a super short pitch (3 to 30 words)\, 3 to 7 images\, an optional 1-minut
 e video\, a portfolio link and contact information. Two-round selection (o
 nline application then interview)\; winners notified by November 2026. Dea
 dline 28 September 2026 at midnight CET.\n\nOrganisation: Ars Electronica 
 and Ceske Budejovice - European Capital of Culture 2028\nLocation: Ceske B
 udejovice\, Czech Republic. Open to applicants of any nationality aged 18+
  who are legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. Required on-site s
 tays: up to one month in February/March 2027\, an optional stay in August/
 September 2027\, and March 2028 for installation\, exhibition and deinstal
 lation.\nAward: EUR 24\,000 total package per winner: EUR 6\,000 artist fe
 e\, up to EUR 15\,000 production budget\, and up to EUR 3\,000 for travel\
 , accommodation and subsistence\, plus a separate budget covering one mont
 h of housing in Ceske Budejovice. Two positions available. Free to apply.\
 nDuration: January 2027 to April 2028\, with on-site blocks in early 2027 
 and March 2028\n\nMore: https://ars.electronica.art/export/en/ecoc2028-ars
 -biologica-art-science-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
URL:https://ars.electronica.art/export/en/ecoc2028-ars-biologica-art-scienc
 e-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Ceske Budejovice\, Czech Republic. Open to applicants of any natio
 nality aged 18+ who are legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. Req
 uired on-site stays: up to one month in February/March 2027\, an optional 
 stay in August/September 2027\, and March 2028 for installation\, exhibiti
 on and deinstallation.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:knowcase-sonar-cotec-open-call-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090441Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:Deadline: KNOWCASE Open Call 2027: permanent digital-culture instal
 lation in Madrid (Sonar+D x Fundacion Cotec)
DESCRIPTION:Fundacion Cotec (a Spanish innovation-focused non-profit) and S
 onar+D\, building on an initial experiment at Sonar 2026\, invite proposal
 s for a long-term installation at KNOWCASE\, the street-level showcase of 
 Cotec's Madrid headquarters in Chamberi\, visible to the public 24 hours a
  day. THEME: digital culture\, meaning projects that combine knowledge\, r
 esearch\, science and data with art\, technology and social impact. Cotec'
 s stated priorities are the big transitions (analogue to digital\, linear 
 to circular economy\, tangible to intangible) plus innovation ecosystems a
 nd R&D&I\, the climate crisis\, the future of work and learning\, social d
 ivides\, citizen participation and democratic health. WHAT YOU GET: a budg
 et of up to EUR 100\,000 plus VAT covering everything (design\, production
 \, installation\, maintenance\, documentation\, artist fees\, travel\, acc
 ommodation and insurance)\, and a minimum 24-month public display from Feb
 ruary 2027. WHO CAN APPLY: artists\, collectives\, designers\, technologis
 ts\, universities\, research centres\, startups\, businesses\, creative ag
 encies\, producers\, cultural institutions and multidisciplinary teams\, i
 nternationally\; applicants must demonstrate solid experience and a releva
 nt portfolio. CONSTRAINTS: the proposal must be original and never previou
 sly exhibited\, must include participative or interactive elements\, must 
 encourage critical thinking\, and must be technically and financially viab
 le for the full 24+ month exhibition period (durability and maintenance ar
 e central selection criteria). Selection weighs conceptual quality highest
  (40 percent)\, followed by public interactivity (25 percent). HOW TO APPL
 Y: one PDF of maximum 15 pages containing summary\, concept\, technical pr
 oposal\, visuals\, budget\, timeline\, team profile\, portfolio and declar
 ation\, in Spanish\, another official language of Spain\, or English\, via
  the application portal linked from the terms and conditions. Results anno
 unced 14 October 2026. Deadline 7 September 2026 at midnight CEST.\n\nOrga
 nisation: Fundacion Cotec and Sonar+D\nLocation: Installation at the KNOWC
 ASE storefront of Fundacion Cotec's headquarters in Chamberi\, Madrid\, Sp
 ain\, a street-level venue visible 24/7. The call is international\; appli
 cations accepted in Spanish\, other official languages of Spain\, or Engli
 sh.\nAward: Up to EUR 100\,000 (plus VAT)\, all-inclusive: conceptualisati
 on\, design\, technical development\, production\, installation\, maintena
 nce\, documentation\, artist fees\, travel\, accommodation and insurance.\
 nDuration: Installation displayed for a minimum of 24 months\, starting Fe
 bruary 2027\n\nMore: https://sonar.es/en/news/knowcase-open-call-2027?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://sonar.es/en/news/knowcase-open-call-2027?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Installation at the KNOWCASE storefront of Fundacion Cotec's headq
 uarters in Chamberi\, Madrid\, Spain\, a street-level venue visible 24/7. 
 The call is international\; applications accepted in Spanish\, other offic
 ial languages of Spain\, or English.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:american-academy-berlin-prize-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090441Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: American Academy in Berlin: Berlin Prize Fellowship 2027-
 28
DESCRIPTION:The American Academy in Berlin is an independent\, nonprofit in
 stitute for advanced study in the arts\, humanities\, social sciences\, la
 w\, journalism and public policy\, whose primary mission is to strengthen 
 the cultural and intellectual ties between the United States and Germany. 
 Each year\, after a rigorous selection process\, the Academy awards roughl
 y two dozen semester-long Berlin Prize fellowships to individuals who demo
 nstrate excellence in their fields. DISCIPLINES: applications are welcome 
 across the humanities\, social sciences\, law\, journalism and various are
 as of public policy. The Academy generally encourages projects addressing 
 pressing issues of the day\, specifically naming democracy\, security\, te
 chnology and society (especially AI)\, energy including climate\, and glob
 al order. Dedicated fellowships also support projects in Jewish studies\, 
 German studies\, and public health and biotechnology. Project proposals ne
 ed not focus on Germany\, but candidates should explain how a residency in
  Berlin would enhance their work. NOT ACCEPTED: proposals in mathematics o
 r the natural sciences. NOTE FOR ARTISTS: visual artists and composers are
  invited through separate\, nomination-based competitions\, so there is no
  open application route for them here. ELIGIBILITY: fellowships are restri
 cted to citizens and permanent residents of the United States. Both emergi
 ng and established figures are eligible. PhD holders qualify as emerging s
 cholars with a strong record of peer-reviewed work beyond the dissertation
 \; candidates in non-academic fields such as journalism\, filmmaking and p
 olicy need a significant record of publication or production\; fiction and
  nonfiction writers must have published at least one book with a reputable
  press. WHAT FELLOWS DO: fellows present their work to audiences in and ar
 ound Berlin through lectures\, readings\, public discussions\, performance
 s and film screenings\, and should be willing to engage with relevant Germ
 an institutions\, organizations and the media\; these activities are integ
 ral to the Academy's mission of fostering transatlantic dialogue. APPLICAT
 ION MATERIALS: a project proposal of 1\,500 to 1\,750 words plus a one-pag
 e bibliography\; a curriculum vitae of up to 10 pages\; two writing sample
 s totalling no more than 60 pages\; and three letters of recommendation\, 
 which are due 2 October 2026. DEADLINE: Friday 25 September 2026 at 12:00 
 noon Eastern Time (18:00 Central European Time). TIMELINE: all candidates 
 are notified of the competition outcome by email in late March 2027\; the 
 2027-28 Berlin Prize recipients are announced in May 2027.\n\nOrganisation
 : American Academy in Berlin\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arn
 hold Center. RESTRICTED: fellowships are open only to citizens and permane
 nt residents of the United States.\nAward: Monthly stipend of USD 5\,000\,
  plus roundtrip airfare\, accommodation at or near the Hans Arnhold Center
 \, partial board\, in-house library services and professional networking o
 pportunities. Roughly two dozen fellowships awarded each year. No applicat
 ion fee.\nDuration: One full academic semester (about 4 months). Fall runs
  mid-August to mid-December\; spring runs late January to late May.\n\nMor
 e: https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arnhold Center. RESTRICTED: fellows
 hips are open only to citizens and permanent residents of the United State
 s.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cultuurloket-digitall-implementatiebijdrage-2026@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090441Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: Cultuurloket DigitALL: Implementation Contribution (Imple
 mentatiebijdrage\, 2026)
DESCRIPTION:A one-off 2026 call letting Dutch cultural institutions reuse a
 nd adapt digital technology that other institutions have already built and
  published in the open DigitALL Toolkit\, for example translation software
  shared between theatres or a streaming platform adapted for a new venue. 
 Grants run up to EUR 30\,000 and cover technical modification\, integratio
 n\, production and deployment costs. Eligible applicants are museums\, the
 atres\, music venues\, festivals\, film theatres and professional performi
 ng arts producers. Applications are submitted online and assessed in order
  of receipt rather than comparatively\, with a decision within ten weeks. 
 The deadline was extended from 31 May 2026 to 1 November 2026 after high d
 emand. IMPORTANT RESTRICTION: this route cannot be used to further develop
  your own pilot internally\; it exists specifically to spread another inst
 itution's working technology across the sector. For developing something n
 ew\, use the DigitALL pilot contribution instead.\n\nOrganisation: Cultuur
 loket DigitALL\nLocation: Netherlands and the Caribbean parts of the Kingd
 om. No city or region restriction.\nAward: Up to EUR 30\,000. Covers techn
 ical modification\, system integration\, production and deployment costs. 
 Hardware purchase alone is ineligible unless clearly essential to the publ
 ic experience. No stated co-financing percentage. No application fee.\nDur
 ation: Not specified\; scoped to a single implementation.\n\nMore: https:/
 /cultuurloketdigitall.nl/implementatiebijdrage/?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://cultuurloketdigitall.nl/implementatiebijdrage/?utm_source=artif
 icialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Netherlands and the Caribbean parts of the Kingdom. No city or reg
 ion restriction.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:european-ai-society-fund-building-narrative-power-2026@artificialnouvea
 u.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090441Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Building Narrative Power: funding and learning programme
DESCRIPTION:The European AI & Society Fund is launching Building Narrative 
 Power\, a EUR 2 million funding and learning programme to counter Big Tech
 's dominance over how AI is understood\, and to help people and communitie
 s shape new ways of thinking and talking about the technology they want. T
 he Fund's strategy is to build the power of the public interest community 
 to shape the direction of AI in Europe\; narratives are central to creatin
 g the conditions for a greater say over how AI is adopted and used. THREE 
 THEMES where research shows the public already feels strongly: (1) DATA CE
 NTRES: as the EU aims to triple data centre numbers in the next five to se
 ven years\, pollution\, energy prices and compromised grid access have mob
 ilised communities across the political spectrum\; proposals should explor
 e narrative questions that move communities beyond resistance towards enga
 gement in shaping data centre expansion. (2) AI AND JOBS: 57% of the publi
 c in a recent study expressed concern about how AI will change employment\
 , more pronounced among lower-income people\, and the concern is not only 
 displacement but the quality and nature of work\, autonomy and dignity\; p
 roposals should move the debate from generalised anxiety into a mobilising
  issue that shapes the future of work. (3) YOUNG PEOPLE: beyond the stride
 nt social media ban debate\, young people encounter AI in education\, frie
 ndships and future careers\, and their wider views are rarely heard\; prop
 osals should build narratives that help young people shape the technologie
 s that will influence their lives. LEARNING PROGRAMME: grantees join a com
 munity of practice\, sharing experiences across issues and places\, suppor
 ting each other through delivery and reflecting on which strategies and st
 ories have most salience\, with findings feeding back to the Fund and othe
 r funders. ABOUT THE FUNDER: the European AI & Society Fund is a pooled fu
 nd with 18 funding partners that mobilises a public interest community in 
 Europe to fight for AI that serves people\, society and planet\; it takes 
 no EU\, government or Big Tech money. ELIGIBILITY: this call is only open 
 to organisations in Austria\, Belgium\, Bulgaria\, Croatia\, the Republic 
 of Cyprus\, Czechia\, Denmark\, Estonia\, Finland\, France\, Germany\, Gre
 ece\, Hungary\, Iceland\, Ireland\, Italy\, Latvia\, Liechtenstein\, Lithu
 ania\, Luxembourg\, Malta\, the Netherlands\, Norway\, Poland\, Portugal\,
  Romania\, Slovakia\, Slovenia\, Spain\, Sweden and Switzerland\; applicat
 ions from the UK\, the Western Balkans and other non-European countries ar
 e not accepted. Guidance for applicants and a full application-form previe
 w are published so you can prepare a draft before submitting. HOW TO APPLY
 : create an account on the Fund's application platform Optimy (link in the
  guidance documents) and submit the first-stage application before 31 Augu
 st 2026. KEY DATES: call launched 16 July 2026\; information sessions 22 J
 uly\, 29 July and 24 August 2026 (11:00 to 12:00 CET)\; first-stage deadli
 ne 31 August 2026\; unsuccessful applicants notified 16 October 2026\; sho
 rtlisted applicants invited to submit a detailed proposal on 19 October 20
 26\, due 16 November 2026\; funding decisions notified 14 December 2026\; 
 grants start February 2027. Questions to info@europeanaifund.org\; an FAQ 
 covering eligibility\, coalitions\, non-profit status and what the grant c
 an be spent on is published at the bottom of the programme page.\n\nOrgani
 sation: European AI & Society Fund\nLocation: Organisations registered in 
 Austria\, Belgium\, Bulgaria\, Croatia\, Republic of Cyprus\, Czechia\, De
 nmark\, Estonia\, Finland\, France\, Germany\, Greece\, Hungary\, Iceland\
 , Ireland\, Italy\, Latvia\, Liechtenstein\, Lithuania\, Luxembourg\, Malt
 a\, Netherlands\, Norway\, Poland\, Portugal\, Romania\, Slovakia\, Sloven
 ia\, Spain\, Sweden or Switzerland. The UK\, the Western Balkans and other
  non-European countries are not eligible for this programme.\nAward: Grant
 s of up to EUR 150\,000 from a EUR 2 million programme\, plus a place in t
 he accompanying learning programme and community of practice. Payments are
  usually 50% on grant signing\, 40% midway through the grant period subjec
 t to previous funds having been spent\, and 10% on completion. As a genera
 l rule organisations should not request more than their previous annual bu
 dget unless the need is motivated and demonstrated\; without a previous an
 nual budget\, request an amount proportional to the project. No applicatio
 n fee.\nDuration: Project grant with an accompanying learning programme\; 
 grants start February 2027\n\nMore: https://europeanaifund.org/newspublica
 tions/call-for-proposals-new-e2m-building-narrative-power-funding-and-lear
 ning-programme/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://europeanaifund.org/newspublications/call-for-proposals-new-e2m-
 building-narrative-power-funding-and-learning-programme/?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Organisations registered in Austria\, Belgium\, Bulgaria\, Croatia
 \, Republic of Cyprus\, Czechia\, Denmark\, Estonia\, Finland\, France\, G
 ermany\, Greece\, Hungary\, Iceland\, Ireland\, Italy\, Latvia\, Liechtens
 tein\, Lithuania\, Luxembourg\, Malta\, Netherlands\, Norway\, Poland\, Po
 rtugal\, Romania\, Slovakia\, Slovenia\, Spain\, Sweden or Switzerland. Th
 e UK\, the Western Balkans and other non-European countries are not eligib
 le for this programme.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:aixdesign-kb-national-library-ai-exhibition-2026@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090441Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: AIxDESIGN x KB: Open Call for Sessions\, (Art)works and P
 erformances
DESCRIPTION:AIxDESIGN and the KB (Koninklijke Bibliotheek\, the National Li
 brary of the Netherlands) are building an exhibition and public programme 
 about AI\, made from the national collection\, at the KB in The Hague. Nad
 ia Piet is Creative Lead with the AIxDESIGN community\, co-presented with 
 the KB. The premise: long before neural nets\, libraries were the machines
  we built to organise human knowledge\, and the show uses the KB's collect
 ion to look at what AI is\, where it comes from\, and how it connects to t
 he older story of how we store\, sort\, and search what we know. Part of t
 he show lives in the KB Atelier\, with talks\, workshops\, and screenings 
 alongside\, intended as AI literacy you can wander into. WHAT THEY WANT: w
 ork that engages AI in some way (critical\, creative\, hands-on\, or playf
 ul)\; bonus if it also speaks to archives\, libraries\, or how we store an
 d sort knowledge\, though you do not need to touch the KB's collection to 
 take part. You may pitch to more than one track: (A) SOMETHING FOR THE OPE
 NING NIGHT\, a performance\, talk\, screening\, tour\, ritual\, booth\, ga
 me\, or other experience for the November 2026 opening\, fee EUR 1\,500 to
  2\,000\; (B) A SESSION IN THE EVENTS PROGRAMME\, a workshop or other sess
 ion once during the three-month run\, around 2.5 to 4 hours\, fee EUR 2\,0
 00 to 2\,500\; (C) AN (ART)WORK FOR THE EXHIBITION\, existing work in any 
 format (installation\, sculpture\, zine stand\, a corner of your own) stur
 dy enough for a public area with daily traffic\, honorarium EUR 200 to 400
  including licensing and production\; (D) SOMETHING ELSE ENTIRELY\, pitch 
 it anyway. WHO: artists\, designers\, researchers\, technologists\, perfor
 mers\, musicians\, archivists\, or educators working around AI\, data\, li
 braries\, or cultural heritage\; no finished idea or polished CV needed\, 
 just a clear\, specific pitch. You can be based anywhere and they especial
 ly welcome people outside the Netherlands\; the show is in-person in The H
 ague and the fee stays the same wherever you are coming from. HOW TO APPLY
 : send a short pitch through the form (which track\, your idea in a paragr
 aph or two\, roughly what it would need\, and a link to your work)\; takes
  about five minutes\, no full application required. Deadline: end of Augus
 t 2026. Questions to hello@aixdesign.co.\n\nOrganisation: AIxDESIGN\, co-p
 resented with the Koninklijke Bibliotheek (KB)\, National Library of the N
 etherlands\nLocation: KB\, The Hague\, Netherlands (in-person exhibition a
 nd programme)\; pitches welcome from people based anywhere\nAward: Fees by
  track: opening-night experience EUR 1\,500 to 2\,000\; a session in the e
 vents programme EUR 2\,000 to 2\,500\; an existing (art)work for the exhib
 ition EUR 200 to 400 honorarium (including licensing and production). The 
 fee stays the same wherever you are based.\nDuration: Exhibition runs Nove
 mber 2026 to February 2027\; opening event November 2026\n\nMore: https://
 nadiapiet.notion.site/Open-Call-AIxDESIGN-x-KB-Sessions-Art-works-More-38f
 37d0038338102b128c57cbeb16d2d?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_me
 dium=referral
URL:https://nadiapiet.notion.site/Open-Call-AIxDESIGN-x-KB-Sessions-Art-wor
 ks-More-38f37d0038338102b128c57cbeb16d2d?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:KB\, The Hague\, Netherlands (in-person exhibition and programme)\
 ; pitches welcome from people based anywhere
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ddc-trygfonden-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090441Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Digital Democracy Centre x TrygFonden Fellowship Program 
 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Digital Democracy Centre (DDC) at the University of Souther
 n Denmark\, with support from TrygFonden\, is calling for applications for
  its 2027 fellowship cohort. The program fosters collaboration between res
 earchers\, journalists\, and policymakers exploring the impact of digital 
 technology and AI on media\, politics\, and democracy. Fellows collaborate
  with a self-selected DDC mentor to generate new knowledge\, contribute to
  public debate\, develop networks\, and produce tangible outreach outputs 
 such as policy briefs\, educational packages\, and talks. THREE FELLOWSHIP
  TYPES: (1) academic fellows researching digital technologies and democrac
 y\; (2) journalist fellows covering digital technology and democracy\; (3)
  policy fellows contributing to the regulatory debate. Target group: resea
 rchers\, journalism graduates\, and policymakers in research/educational i
 nstitutions\, media organizations\, ministries\, NGOs\, etc. PRIORITIES: p
 rojects with clear relevance to Denmark or Danish society (including compa
 rative/international projects yielding insights relevant to a Danish conte
 xt)\; in 2027\, projects related to DDC's Hybrid Warfare Initiative (socie
 tal resilience to new forms of international conflict\, with a focus on di
 gital technologies and AI) are also prioritized. EXPECTATIONS: applicants 
 must secure confirmation of mentorship from their desired DDC mentor BEFOR
 E applying (applications without a confirmed mentor are automatically reje
 cted\; PhD students and research assistants are generally not eligible as 
 mentors\, and Sten Schaumburg-Muller is unavailable as a mentor for the 20
 27 call)\; applicants must also coordinate support from their current supe
 rvisors/managers. Fellows engage in DDC activities\, seminars/workshops\, 
 and public outreach\, and submit a short report within two months of finis
 hing. APPLICATION (in English): preliminary project title\, fellowship typ
 e\, brief project description (max 1000 chars)\, detailed description and 
 timeline (max 2400 chars\, including duration\, confirmed mentor\, and rem
 ote/in-office work plan)\, description of activities and collaboration wit
 h external practice partners (max 1000 chars)\, expected product/impact/ou
 tput (max 500 chars)\, a CV (max 5 pages)\, and an overview of the posts f
 or which financial support is sought. Submit the application form (PDF or 
 Word) plus CV as PDFs to ddc@sdu.dk with subject line 'DDC / TrygFonden Fe
 llowship application'. Deadline 1 September 2026\; decisions communicated 
 no later than 1 October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Digital Democracy Centre (U
 niversity of Southern Denmark)\, with support from TrygFonden\nLocation: O
 dense\, Denmark (University of Southern Denmark). Flexible mix of in-offic
 e stays at the DDC in Odense and remote periods\; stays planned to the Dan
 ish working calendar.\nAward: Either a salary subsidy or a stipend to help
  with travel and/or housing\; specific benefits (financial support\, acces
 s to DDC research resources\, flexible working spaces\, speaker series\, n
 etworks) vary by agreement between the fellow\, DDC\, and partnering organ
 izations. Applicants describe the posts they seek support for (flights\, h
 ousing\, research costs\, salary compensation\, stipend\, etc.).\nDuration
 : Flexible\; typically 2-6 months (minimum two months\, can include shorte
 r DDC stays and remote periods)\, longer in exceptional cases. Fellowships
  start anywhere in 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.sdu.dk/en/forskning/forsknin
 gsenheder/samf/digitaldemocracycentre/news+folder/ddcxtrygfonden-fellowshi
 p-call-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.sdu.dk/en/forskning/forskningsenheder/samf/digitaldemocracy
 centre/news+folder/ddcxtrygfonden-fellowship-call-2027?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Odense\, Denmark (University of Southern Denmark). Flexible mix of
  in-office stays at the DDC in Odense and remote periods\; stays planned t
 o the Danish working calendar.
END:VEVENT
